{"date": "2026-11-15", "post_id": 1511419, "title": "Leonid Meteors Through Orion", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-15-leonid-meteors-through-orion/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Where will the next meteor appear? Even during a meteor shower, it is practically impossible to know. Therefore, a good way to enjoy a <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/meteor-shower/\">meteor shower</a> is to find a place where you can sit comfortably and monitor a great expanse of dark sky. And it may be satisfying to share this experience with a friend. The meteor shower depicted was the 2022 <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/meteors-meteorites/leonids/\">Leonids</a> and the view is from <a href=\"https://youtu.be/GANaIlOIjvA\">Hainan</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China\">China</a> looking out over the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea\">South China Sea</a>. Meteor streaks captured over a few hours were isolated and added to a foreground image recorded earlier. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-1998-december-08-leonids-from-leo/\">Leonid meteor streaks</a> trace back to the <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/constellations/leo-heres-your-constellation/\">constellation of Leo</a> and were here seen <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2018-november-23-good-morning-leonid/\">streaking across</a> the constellation of Orion. The bright red <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/\">planet Mars</a> appears near the top of the image. The <a href=\"https://www.planetary.org/articles/your-guide-meteor-shower\">Leonids peak</a> in mid-November every year and so will <a href=\"https://www.cuteness.com/img/gallery/15-funny-photos-that-are-peak-cat/intro-1762504825.webp\">peak again</a> this week. Bonding over their love of astronomy, the two pictured meteor enthusiasts, shown celebrating their <a href=\"https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/bring-science-home-probability-birthday-paradox/\">common birthday</a> this month, were later married.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong><a href=\"https://apod.com/fap/ap261116.html\">open space</a>", "credit": "<a href=\"mailto:mgsluo at gmail dot com\">Luo Hongyang</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"mailto:mgsluo at gmail dot com\">Luo Hongyang</a>", "alt": "A composite showing many meteors trails streaking across a sky featuring the familiar constellation of Orion. In the foreground two people sit in adjoining chairs facing away from the camera, one holding a wand with a glowing star at the end. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-15-leonid-meteors-through-orion/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Leonids2022_Hongyang_2880.jpg?w=2880&h=3600&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 15 - Leonid Meteors Through Orion</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-15-leonid-meteors-through-orion/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Where will the next meteor appear? Even during a meteor shower, it is practically impossible to know. Therefore, a good way to enjoy a meteor shower is to find a place where you can sit comfortably and monitor a great expanse of dark sky. And it may be satisfying to share this experience with a friend. The meteor shower depicted was the 2022 Leonids and the view is from Hainan, China looking out over the South China Sea. Meteor streaks captured over a few hours were isolated and added to a foreground image recorded earlier. Leonid meteor streaks trace back to the constellation of Leo and were here seen streaking across the constellation of Orion. The bright red planet Mars appears near the top of the image. The Leonids peak in mid-November every year and so will peak again this week. Bonding over their love of astronomy, the two pictured meteor enthusiasts, shown celebrating their common birthday this month, were later married.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"leonid meteors through orion\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Leonid Meteors Through Orion\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-15-leonid-meteors-through-orion/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Leonids2022_Hongyang_2880.jpg?w=2880&#038;h=3600&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Leonids2022_Hongyang_2880.jpg?w=2880&#038;h=3600&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A composite showing many meteors trails streaking across a sky featuring the familiar constellation of Orion. In the foreground two people sit in adjoining chairs facing away from the camera, one holding a wand with a glowing star at the end. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-15T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-13T12:49:05-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Leonid Meteors Through Orion\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Leonids2022_Hongyang_2880.jpg?w=2880&#038;h=3600&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 15<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Leonids2022_Hongyang_2880.jpg\" onMouseOver=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Leonids2022_Hongyang_960_annotated.jpg';\"\nonMouseOut=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Leonids2022_Hongyang_2880.jpg';\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Leonids2022_Hongyang_2880.jpg\" name=imagename1 alt=\"A composite showing many meteors trails streaking across a sky featuring the familiar constellation of Orion. In the foreground two people sit in adjoining chairs facing away from the camera, one holding a wand with a glowing star at the end. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Leonid Meteors Through Orion </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"mailto:mgsluo at gmail dot com\">Luo Hongyang</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Where will the next meteor appear? Even during a meteor shower, it is practically impossible to know. Therefore, a good way to enjoy a <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/meteor-shower/\">meteor shower</a> is to find a place where you can sit comfortably and monitor a great expanse of dark sky. And it may be satisfying to share this experience with a friend. The meteor shower depicted was the 2022 <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/meteors-meteorites/leonids/\">Leonids</a> and the view is from <a href=\"https://youtu.be/GANaIlOIjvA\">Hainan</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China\">China</a> looking out over the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_China_Sea\">South China Sea</a>. Meteor streaks captured over a few hours were isolated and added to a foreground image recorded earlier. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-1998-december-08-leonids-from-leo/\">Leonid meteor streaks</a> trace back to the <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/constellations/leo-heres-your-constellation/\">constellation of Leo</a> and were here seen <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2018-november-23-good-morning-leonid/\">streaking across</a> the constellation of Orion. The bright red <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/\">planet Mars</a> appears near the top of the image. The <a href=\"https://www.planetary.org/articles/your-guide-meteor-shower\">Leonids peak</a> in mid-November every year and so will <a href=\"https://www.cuteness.com/img/gallery/15-funny-photos-that-are-peak-cat/intro-1762504825.webp\">peak again</a> this week. Bonding over their love of astronomy, the two pictured meteor enthusiasts, shown celebrating their <a href=\"https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/bring-science-home-probability-birthday-paradox/\">common birthday</a> this month, were later married.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong><a href=\"https://apod.com/fap/ap261116.html\">open space</a>\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-14-apollo-12-and-surveyor-3/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261115/html"}